g you know it's not  just the lust for the violence and the  power but is that we don't know how to  do it any differently and so to to help  find new experiences and I've been able  to find that you people do that all over  the world they're happy to come together  in new ways to really find the way to to  become human again to connect with their  own spirits and with the spirits of the  people and I'm not sure how my time is I  think I'm just about at an end here no  that's good it's um I mean it is because  the essence of what I wanted to say is  about the hope I mean you know my book  takes you through step-by-step of you  know how you can practice experiential  therapy in a safe way but that's not  really the message I wanted to bring  here today most of you probably aren't  clinicians some of you may be and the  book is helpful just in in being able to  understand about trauma but but the to  leave you with a message that we don't  have to end in despair that as we watch  the images of the violence that will  occur on the television and will be  impacted by that you can get secondary  PTSD just from watching it on the  television you know in many ways we as  Americans or a very privileged society  and a privileged group of people and  I've seen that as I've gone around the  world and seen the lack of of privilege  and I believe we have a responsibility  as a culture to reach out with messages  of hope I mean if we could only spend  the money that we're spending on the  bombs in humanitarian aid and truly make  a difference but we can only do that one  person at a time so each person can take  their own responsibility for finding  those new experiences whether it's  through a therapy model or through a  community practice or through however  you connect is the message that I hope  to leave you with  all right now we have the terrible  quandary of signing or questions and  what do you guys want to do are you  going to tell me what you want to do you  can ask a q