r is over you bring back a culture  of 300,000 soldiers who come back and  caught of that patriotism is a new cycle  that begins again which means that we  just continue in this you touch upon for  me a very important point and you know  when we look at the destruction of war  we always look at the physical  destruction you take Bosnia which i  covered the bridges the buildings the  infrastructure but probably more  dangerous is that unseen psychological  destruction and one of the benefits that  we as a country have from the legacy of  our debacle in Vietnam is that because  it was a defeat we have not permitted  Vietnam veterans to mythologize the war  the way we have with World War two  veterans they they suffer more trauma  because it because of it but they also  speak a lot more truth about war and I  they have a lot to tell us and we're not  listening it has been done since the  Iliad when Nestor got up and talked  about the age of heroes we look back on  past conflicts we elevate and we and  exalt those that carry out carried it  out the greatest generation and what  we're doing in essence is creating in  perpetuating that myth of war I grew up  in a family my father and all my uncle's  fought in the war one of whom for the  South Pacific he was in the regular army  in 1939 eventually was wounded and never  recovered he never was able to hold down  a job he was a burden on our family was  particularly difficult because as an  alcoholic and and we my father mother  did not drink any he drank himself to  death in a trailer and I don't think  that's an uncommon story that's a silent  story that's what war does  so yes we bring back people who are  maimed and brutalized and then we we  discard them and we don't listen to them  war is always about betrayal it is about  betrayal of the young by the old and it  is about betrayal of soldiers by  politicians  hey uh what would you recommend for  people who are what talking to mike its  00 talking to them oh here I'm o